<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Burgundians</h1><p class="st-meta">411 CE – 534 CE · capital Lugdunum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">East Germanic kingdom of the Rhône and Saône valleys, whose destruction on the Rhine inspired the Nibelungenlied and whose laws shaped Burgundy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the early 5th century, and in 411 their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gundahar">Gundahar</a> set up a realm on the Rhine, traditionally placed around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-worms">Worms</a>, as Roman allies. In 436 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> had them crushed by Hunnic troops, a disaster remembered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a>. In 443 the survivors were resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapaudia">Sapaudia</a>, around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From there they spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> valleys, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gundobad">Gundobad</a> issued a law code for his Burgundian subjects and another for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallo-romans">Gallo-Romans</a> shared land. The kings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arians</a>, but Sigismund converted to Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>' sons attacked them in 523 and captured Sigismund, who was put to death in 524. After a final defeat in 534 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godomar-ii">Godomar</a> disappeared and the land became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks</a>, later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy">Frankish Kingdom of Burgundy</a>. The name <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a> has endured for fifteen centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-burgundians.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Burgundians" width="320" height="326"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (Sidonius) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Koenigreich_Burgund_DE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>411 CE – 534 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lugdunum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gundahar, Gundobad, Sigismund</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burgundian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France, Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First Kingdom of Burgundy, Burgundian Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
